# EU Solicit - Epic Breakdown

## Overview

This document provides the complete epic and story breakdown for EU Solicit, decomposing the requirements from the PRD, UX Design if it exists, and Architecture requirements into implementable stories.

## Requirements Inventory

### Functional Requirements

FR1: Ingest AOP, TED, and EU grant portals data into a centralized PostgreSQL 16 data store.
FR2: Smart Matching with AI relevance scoring against user capabilities, certifications, and financial capacities.
FR3: Configurable email digests based on CPV, regions, budget, and deadlines.
FR4: Ingest full tender packages (PDF, DOCX) up to 100MB per file, max 500MB per package.
FR5: Generate one-page executive summaries of complex RFPs.
FR6: Extract mandatory requirements into a compliance checklist.
FR7: Risk Analysis to flag unusual or high-risk contractual clauses.
FR8: Streamed generation of full technical proposals using KraftData vector stores (RAG) and user profiles.
FR9: Predict evaluator scoring and suggest improvements.
FR10: Automated compliance checks against admin-assigned regulatory frameworks (e.g., ZOP).
FR11: Support Subscription Tiers: Free, Starter, Professional, Enterprise tiers gated by Stripe.
FR12: Google and Outlook calendar sync (Pro/Enterprise) plus an iCal feed.
FR13: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) with company role ceiling and entity-level overrides.
FR14: Audit Logging for user actions, IP, before/after values for POST/PATCH/DELETE endpoints.
FR15: AI recommendation on bid viability with admin manual override capability.

### NonFunctional Requirements

NFR1: 99.5% uptime SLA.
NFR2: All AI generation operations must use Server-Sent Events (SSE) with `run-stream`, no blocking waits.
NFR3: SSE endpoints must implement idle timeout (120s), total timeout (600s), and heartbeat (15s).
NFR4: Passwords bounded at `max_length=128` characters.
NFR5: `hmac.compare_digest()` required for all inbound webhook validations.
NFR6: AES-256 for data at rest; TLS 1.3 for transit.
NFR7: `is_active` checked for all token endpoints.
NFR8: Outbound calls must use circuit breakers wrapping exponential backoff.
NFR9: Idempotent Redis Streams consumption via DB constraints + `SETNX`.
NFR10: Atomic Lua scripts for rate/usage limits.
NFR11: Celery background tasks use `SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` for DB queues to prevent race conditions.
NFR12: All data stored within the EU (GDPR compliance).

### Additional Requirements

- Use Next.js 14 App Router with TypeScript (strict mode).
- Use Zustand (namespaced for UI/Auth state) & TanStack Query v5 for state management.
- Use React Hook Form + Zod, shadcn/ui for forms and UI components.
- Use next-intl v3 for i18n (BG/EN).
- Use native `fetch` + `ReadableStream` for SSE streaming POST; Axios is prohibited for SSE.
- Backend must be Python 3.12+ with FastAPI.
- ORM & DB: SQLAlchemy (async), Alembic, PostgreSQL 16.
- Background Tasks: Celery for long-running crawler/NLP tasks.
- Event Bus: Redis 7 for fast stream processing with Lua script support.
- Infrastructure: Docker multi-stage builds, Terraform >= 1.5, Helm 3.
- Observability: structlog (JSON to stdlib routing), Prometheus (`CollectorRegistry`) for latency/business metrics.
- Database relies on strict PostgreSQL schema isolation per service (`client`, `admin`, `pipeline`, `ai`, `notification`, `shared`).
- Outbound external HTTP client calls must implement a two-layer resilience pattern (Circuit Breaker per-logical-name + inner Exponential Retry).
- Webhook deduplication via `event_id` unique constraint in `webhook_events` table inside the processing transaction.
- VIES VAT validation and similar services must fail-open to `pending` status on timeout/error. Stripe customer provisioning must run as a background task.
- `TierGate` must be implemented via `Depends()`, not as global middleware.
- Write audit trails using `asyncio.create_task()` catching all internal exceptions.
- ThreadPoolExecutor & asyncio.to_thread for Synchronous I/O to prevent blocking the main FastAPI event loop.
- Use `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` for DB-driven queues. Use content-hash optimistic locking (SHA-256) for collaborative document editing.

### UX Design Requirements

UX-DR1: Implement a Split-Pane Proposal Editor with left sidebar for document structure, center Tiptap rich-text editing canvas, and right sliding context panel.
UX-DR2: Implement an AI Diff Review Block using side-by-side or inline text view showing deletions in red strike-through and additions in green highlight, with sticky Accept/Reject buttons, avoiding silent overwriting.
UX-DR3: Implement a Compliance Metric Card with a circular progress ring, fractional compliance, and expandable accordion detailing missing mandatory items.
UX-DR4: Use shadcn/ui components (Dialogs, Sheets, Tabs, Dropdowns) installed directly into `src/components/ui` and styled via Tailwind CSS with a strict 4px spacing scale.
UX-DR5: Use `@tanstack/react-table` for highly interactive data tables and `Recharts` for analytics visualizations.
UX-DR6: Support a themeable architecture using CSS custom properties for enterprise white-labeling and native dark mode support.
UX-DR7: Ensure WCAG 2.1 AA compliance including strict color contrast (4.5:1 for text), keyboard navigation with explicit focus rings (`:focus-visible`), and screen reader support (ARIA).
UX-DR8: Implement transient success Toasts (via Sonner), persistent inline Warning banners for critical states, and skeleton loaders matching incoming data shapes.
UX-DR9: Build a responsive Application Shell featuring a collapsible left navigation sidebar, sticky top bar with global Cmd+K search, and an optional right-side Inspector Panel.
UX-DR10: Provide a Wizard Stepper for complex forms like the ESPD builder to prevent cognitive overload.

### FR Coverage Map

FR1: Epic 2 - Ingest AOP, TED, and EU grant portals data
FR2: Epic 2 - Smart Matching with AI relevance scoring
FR3: Epic 2 - Configurable email digests
FR4: Epic 3 - Ingest full tender packages
FR5: Epic 3 - Generate one-page executive summaries
FR6: Epic 3 - Extract mandatory requirements into a compliance checklist
FR7: Epic 3 - Risk Analysis to flag high-risk clauses
FR8: Epic 4 - Streamed generation of full technical proposals
FR9: Epic 4 - Predict evaluator scoring and suggest improvements
FR10: Epic 5 - Automated compliance checks against regulatory frameworks
FR11: Epic 1 - Support Subscription Tiers
FR12: Epic 6 - Google and Outlook calendar sync
FR13: Epic 1 - Role-Based Access Control
FR14: Epic 6 - Audit Logging
FR15: Epic 6 - AI recommendation on bid viability with override

## Epic List

### Epic 1: User Registration & Subscription
Users can create accounts, select a pricing tier, and manage their subscription.
**FRs covered:** FR11, FR13

### Epic 2: Opportunity Intelligence & Discovery
Users can ingest public tender data, receive smart matches based on their profiles, and get configurable email digests.
**FRs covered:** FR1, FR2, FR3

### Epic 3: Document Analysis & Insights
Users can upload large tender packages and receive AI-generated executive summaries, compliance checklists, and risk analyses.
**FRs covered:** FR4, FR5, FR6, FR7

### Epic 4: AI Proposal Generation
Users can stream real-time AI-drafted proposals based on tender requirements, user profiles, and predict evaluator scores.
**FRs covered:** FR8, FR9

### Epic 5: Compliance & Regulatory Validation
Users can automatically validate their drafted proposals against regulatory frameworks to ensure full compliance.
**FRs covered:** FR10

### Epic 6: Workflow, Collaboration & Approvals
Admin users can make bid/no-bid decisions, sync calendars, and the system securely logs all actions for auditability.
**FRs covered:** FR12, FR14, FR15

## Epic 1: User Registration & Subscription

Users can create accounts, select a pricing tier, and manage their subscription.

### Story 1.1: User Registration

As a new user,
I want to register for an account using my email and password,
So that I can access the platform.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** a valid email and password (max 128 chars)
**When** I submit the registration form
**Then** my account is created
**And** password is hashed asynchronously and I am logged in

### Story 1.2: Free Tier Selection

As a registered user,
I want to select the Free tier,
So that I can evaluate the platform.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I am a registered user
**When** I select the Free tier
**Then** my account is provisioned with Free tier access
**And** VIES VAT validation fails-open to pending on timeout

### Story 1.3: Subscription Upgrade

As a free user,
I want to upgrade to Professional tier via Stripe,
So that I can use advanced features.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I am on the Free tier
**When** I choose to upgrade
**Then** a Stripe checkout session is created
**And** upon payment success webhook, my access is instantly updated

### Story 1.4: Role-Based Access Setup

As a company admin,
I want to assign roles to my team members,
So that access is appropriately gated.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I am a company admin
**When** I invite a user and assign a role
**Then** the user receives an invite
**And** their access is restricted by the TierGate injection

## Epic 2: Opportunity Intelligence & Discovery

Users can ingest public tender data, receive smart matches based on their profiles, and get configurable email digests.

### Story 2.1: Data Ingestion Pipeline

As a platform admin,
I want the system to ingest public tender data (AOP, TED),
So that users have opportunities to search.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** active data sources
**When** the Celery crawler job runs
**Then** it ingests tender data into the pipeline schema
**And** uses SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED to prevent race conditions

### Story 2.2: Opportunity Smart Matching

As a Professional user,
I want AI to score opportunities against my profile,
So that I see the most relevant tenders first.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** my user profile is complete
**When** I view the dashboard
**Then** opportunities are sorted by an AI relevance score
**And** displayed in high-density data tables

### Story 2.3: Email Digest Configuration

As a Bid Manager,
I want to configure daily email alerts,
So that I never miss a relevant opportunity.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I have set criteria (CPV, regions)
**When** the daily digest worker runs
**Then** I receive a formatted email with matching opportunities
**And** I can click through directly to the platform

## Epic 3: Document Analysis & Insights

Users can upload large tender packages and receive AI-generated executive summaries, compliance checklists, and risk analyses.

### Story 3.1: Tender Package Upload

As a user,
I want to upload a tender package (up to 500MB total),
So that the system can analyze it.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I have PDF/DOCX files
**When** I upload them
**Then** the system accepts files up to 100MB each
**And** saves them to the data store with an audit log entry

### Story 3.2: Executive Summary Generation

As a user,
I want an AI-generated executive summary of the tender,
So that I can quickly decide if it's worth pursuing.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** an uploaded tender package
**When** I request a summary
**Then** the AI Gateway generates a one-page summary
**And** the text is presented clearly with citations

### Story 3.3: Compliance Checklist Extraction

As a Bid Manager,
I want the system to extract mandatory requirements,
So that I have a clear checklist for compliance.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** an analyzed tender
**When** I view the requirements tab
**Then** a checklist of mandatory items is displayed
**And** I can mark them as complete

### Story 3.4: Risk Clause Flagging

As a Reviewer,
I want the system to flag high-risk clauses,
So that I can mitigate contractual risks early.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** the analyzed tender
**When** the risk analysis runs
**Then** unusual/high-risk clauses are highlighted in the UI
**And** the UI uses persistent inline Warning banners for critical risks

## Epic 4: AI Proposal Generation

Users can stream real-time AI-drafted proposals based on tender requirements, user profiles, and predict evaluator scores.

### Story 4.1: Proposal Draft Initialization

As a Contributor,
I want to start a new proposal draft using a template,
So that I don't have to start from scratch.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I am in a Project Workspace
**When** I click "Start Proposal"
**Then** a Split-Pane Proposal Editor is opened
**And** it includes a left sidebar, center Tiptap canvas, and right context panel

### Story 4.2: SSE Streamed Proposal Drafting

As a Contributor,
I want the AI to stream the draft proposal text into the editor,
So that I can see the content as it's generated.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I request AI drafting for a section
**When** the backend processes it
**Then** the text is streamed via SSE using ReadableStream
**And** AI Diff Review Block allows me to accept/reject changes

### Story 4.3: Evaluator Score Prediction

As a Bid Manager,
I want to see a predicted evaluator score for my draft,
So that I can improve weak sections.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** a drafted proposal
**When** I run the scoring simulator
**Then** it returns a predicted score
**And** provides specific improvement suggestions in the right inspector panel

## Epic 5: Compliance & Regulatory Validation

Users can automatically validate their drafted proposals against regulatory frameworks to ensure full compliance.

### Story 5.1: Automated Compliance Check

As a Bid Manager,
I want the system to automatically validate my proposal against the ZOP framework,
So that I don't miss any legal requirements.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** a completed draft
**When** I run the compliance check
**Then** the Compliance Metric Card shows fractional compliance
**And** expands to detail any missing mandatory items

## Epic 6: Workflow, Collaboration & Approvals

Admin users can make bid/no-bid decisions, sync calendars, and the system securely logs all actions for auditability.

### Story 6.1: Bid/No-Bid Decision

As an Admin,
I want to record the bid or no-bid decision with a manual override,
So that the team knows whether to proceed.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** the AI recommendation
**When** I select a decision
**Then** it is recorded independently of the AI recommendation
**And** written to audit logs asynchronously

### Story 6.2: Calendar Synchronization

As a Pro user,
I want my proposal deadlines synced to Google/Outlook,
So that I don't miss submission dates.

**Acceptance Criteria:**

**Given** I connect my calendar
**When** a deadline is set in the workspace
**Then** it is automatically synced to my external calendar
**And** it triggers Transient Success Toasts upon successful sync
